{
  "rules": [
    "Strong upward-trend underprediction: when the last ~5–10 days show a sustained rise, the model lags and stays too low across the whole horizon; apply an additive uplift that grows with horizon (small at days 1–3, larger by days 10–30).",
    "Level bias in rallies: in bullish regimes the forecast path is too flat (insufficient slope). Increase the daily drift (slope) rather than only shifting the level, especially for days 5–20.",
    "Horizon-dependent error: underestimation increases with lead time (errors are modest early, large mid/late horizon). Use a ramped correction (e.g., +X at day 1 increasing to +Y by day 30) instead of a constant offset.",
    "Post-spike mean-reversion overshoot: after a sharp jump in history, the model tends to revert/soften too quickly versus reality (true series holds gains and continues higher). Reduce mean-reversion strength for 1–3 weeks after upside breaks.",
    "Volatility compression: forecasts systematically understate amplitude (both peaks and persistence). Expand deviations from the recent mean/last price (i.e., apply a volatility/elasticity multiplier > 1 in trending periods).",
    "Late-horizon turning-point miss: when the true series eventually rolls over (example ending), the model still remains too low beforehand but also fails to anticipate the eventual dip timing; keep uplift through ~day 20 but taper corrections after ~day 25 if recent momentum weakens."
  ]
}
